WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:05.182
Logical execution order explains many SQL rules that
can otherwise feel arbitrary

00:00:06.923 --> 00:00:11.515
PostgreSQL conceptually begins with FROM and JOIN
then applies WHERE forms groups

00:00:11.528 --> 00:00:16.475
with GROUP BY filters groups with HAVING evaluates
SELECT expressions performs window

00:00:16.488 --> 00:00:21.686
calculations and then applies ORDER BY and LIMIT
This is why a

00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:26.570
same-level aggregate is not available in WHERE The
groups do not exist

00:00:26.596 --> 00:00:32.221
yet at that stage HAVING can use aggregate
expressions because grouping

00:00:32.248 --> 00:00:38.121
has already happened Window functions are evaluated
after grouped filtering

00:00:38.411 --> 00:00:41.516
which is why they can add analytical context to the
rows that

00:00:41.529 --> 00:00:47.477
remain You do not need to memorize every internal
implementation detail

00:00:48.401 --> 00:00:52.414
Use this logical order as a reasoning model for
understanding which clause

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.638
can see which values
